     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 0,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": 0,
     "spaceBefore": 6,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": null
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Aspects (abnormal in red)"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Typical changes may not become visible on radiographs for years; MRI shows changes earlier (early inflammatory changes, "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       },
       {
        "type": "highlight",
        "attrs": {
         "hex": "FF0000"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "bone marrow edema on T2-weighted images"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " with fatty changes, subtle structural abnormalities), but there is no consensus on its role in routine diagnosis; pelvic MRI should be done if suspicion of spondyloarthritis is high or other causes must be excluded; routine monitoring with serial spine radiographs is not recommended"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Labs: generally ESR, CRP, and CBC; HLA-B27 is present in 90% of White patients but also in up to 10% of the general population depending on ethnicity; no laboratory test is diagnostic; if still suspected, radiography or MRI of the lumbosacral spine and sacroiliac joints, where "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       },
       {
        "type": "highlight",
        "attrs": {
         "hex": "FF0000"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "sacroiliitis on radiograph"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " strongly supports the diagnosis"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 0,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": 0,
     "spaceBefore": 6,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": null
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Causes"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "The prototypical spondyloarthropathy: a systemic disorder with inflammation of the axial skeleton, large peripheral joints, and digits; nocturnal back pain; morning back stiffness; accentuated kyphosis; constitutional symptoms; aortitis; cardiac conduction abnormalities; and anterior uveitis"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "3 times more frequent in men than in women; begins most often between ages 20 and 40; 10 to 20 times more common among first-degree relatives; HLA-B27 present in 90% of White patients (and up to 10% of the general population, depending on ethnicity); identical-twin concordance only approximately 50%, suggesting environmental factors contribute"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 0,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": 0,
     "spaceBefore": 6,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": null
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Symptoms"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Back pain (often nocturnal, of varying intensity) eventually becomes recurrent; morning stiffness (typically relieved by activity) and paraspinal muscle spasm develop; kyphosis is common in untreated patients; severe hip and shoulder arthritis can eventually develop; in late stages, untreated patients may develop accentuated kyphosis, loss of lumbar lordosis, and fixed bent-forward posturing; there may be peripheral joint involvement, sometimes dactylitis; Achilles and patellar tendinitis can occur"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Systemic manifestations in approximately 25%: recurrent acute anterior uveitis is common; neurologic signs occasionally (compression radiculitis or sciatica, vertebral fracture or subluxation, cauda equina syndrome); cardiovascular: aortic insufficiency, aortitis, angina, pericarditis, and cardiac conduction abnormalities (which may be asymptomatic); nonspecific colitis has been noted in 30%"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Suspect in patients, particularly young men, with nocturnal back pain plus prolonged morning stiffness and kyphosis, diminished chest expansion, Achilles or patellar tendinitis, or unexplained anterior uveitis; a first-degree relative with ankylosing spondylitis should raise suspicion"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 0,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": 0,
     "spaceBefore": 6,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": null
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Treatment and next step"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "NSAIDs are first-line therapy for most patients: they reduce pain and suppress joint inflammation and muscle spasm, increasing range of motion; the dose should be as low as possible, but maximum doses may be needed with active disease"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Biologic therapy when NSAIDs give inadequate control: TNF inhibitors (eg, etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab) are often strikingly effective for inflammatory back pain; adalimumab or infliximab are preferred over etanercept with recurrent uveitis; if a TNF inhibitor is ineffective, switching to an IL-17 inhibitor is preferred to another TNF inhibitor"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Use TNF inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, or JAK inhibitors in patients who respond inadequately to NSAIDs"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Daily exercise and supportive measures (eg, postural training, therapeutic exercise) are vital, strengthening the extensor rather than flexor muscles; smoking cessation should be encouraged (associated with increased pain and disease activity)"
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
